WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.060
- Standard community property applies,

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.150
and she is entitled to
half of the marital assets,

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.130
or $11.395 million.

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:08.920
Jordan fades back, swish!

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.098
And that's the game!

00:00:11.098 --> 00:00:13.560
- Yeah, that's not the end of the game.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.769
That's the beginning.

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:18.041
(light music)

00:00:18.041 --> 00:00:21.120
Hey, legal eagles, D. James Stone here

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.980
teaching you how to think like a lawyer

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.802
so you can crush law school.

00:00:24.802 --> 00:00:26.820
You know, when I'm not
teaching law students

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.610
how to kick ass in law school,

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.160
I'm often watching
courtroom movies myself.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.022
But unlike all of you normals,

00:00:33.022 --> 00:00:35.950
I can't turn off that
lawyer part of my brain

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.016
that's always analyzing
the courtroom scenes

00:00:38.016 --> 00:00:40.349
for whether they're using a great tactic

00:00:40.349 --> 00:00:42.148
that I should use in my own practice

00:00:42.148 --> 00:00:45.670
or whether I should just
pull out all of my hair.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:48.857
Last time around I analyzed
a few of my favorite

00:00:48.857 --> 00:00:50.276
courtroom movies.

00:00:50.276 --> 00:00:53.870
This time around I'm reacting
to some of the legal movies

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.100
that you suggested that I watch.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.314
If you have additional ideas
for movies that I haven't

00:00:59.314 --> 00:01:01.860
reacted to yet, please let
me know in the Comments.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.460
I'm always looking for new ideas

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.886
and other legal movies that
I've never seen before.

00:01:05.886 --> 00:01:07.470
But let's dive right in.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.258
We're gonna start with one of the funniest

00:01:09.258 --> 00:01:11.310
courtroom scenes of all time,

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:12.811
and that's in Liar Liar.

00:01:12.811 --> 00:01:15.930
- Mr. Reede, it is out
of sheer morbid curiosity

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.543
I'm allowing this freak show to continue.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:22.523
Mrs. Cole.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.130
If you dare.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.610
- Mrs. Cole...

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.383
Is this a copy of your driver's license?

00:01:32.698 --> 00:01:33.883
- Yes.

00:01:34.937 --> 00:01:35.770
- That's good.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.560
He's laying the foundation
with the witness.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:39.743
It's a simple question,

00:01:39.743 --> 00:01:43.930
but you have to establish
that a document is

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:45.350
what it purports to be,

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.080
and he can do that through this woman

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.810
who owns the driver's license.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.640
So that's good foundation work.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.529
- It says here you're a blonde, are you?

00:01:54.529 --> 00:01:55.362
- Gah.

00:01:55.362 --> 00:01:57.132
- If you don't remember,
perhaps Mr. Falk will.

00:01:57.132 --> 00:01:58.420
- [Mrs. Cole] Brunette.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.360
- We can play the tape again.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.193
Maybe it's on there.

00:02:00.193 --> 00:02:01.783
- I'm a brunette!

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.190
- Thank you.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.600
Now let's see.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.390
Weight 105.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:08.223
Yeah.

00:02:08.223 --> 00:02:09.624
In your bra.

00:02:09.624 --> 00:02:10.565
- Your honor, I object.

00:02:10.565 --> 00:02:11.398
- You would.

00:02:11.398 --> 00:02:12.231
- Bastard!

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:13.114
- Hag!

00:02:13.114 --> 00:02:13.947
- Quiet!

00:02:13.947 --> 00:02:15.700
- Now normally you wouldn't try to set up

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.296
your own client and witness
to be shown to be a liar.

00:02:19.296 --> 00:02:23.038
But this is all part of
sort of an elaborate ruse,

00:02:23.038 --> 00:02:25.640
so it obviously leads somewhere.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.821
But generally not the
best practice to impugn

00:02:27.821 --> 00:02:32.000
the reputation of your
own witness and client.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.200
- Overruled.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.033
Weight.

00:02:35.597 --> 00:02:36.913
- 118.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.130
All right, fine.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.200
Fine, I'm 127.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.033
- Uh-huh.

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:44.210
And it says here you were born in 1964,

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.920
but that's not true either, is it?

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.310
Is it?

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.143
- No.

00:02:48.143 --> 00:02:49.636
- Now let me get this straight.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:52.629
That would mean that
you lied about your age

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:55.360
to make yourself older.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.683
But why would any woman wanna do that?

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.500
- I changed it so I could get married.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.010
- And the truth

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:05.593
shall set you free!

00:03:07.277 --> 00:03:09.620
My client lied about her age.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.510
She was only 17 when she got married,

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.510
which makes her a minor.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.030
And in the great state of California,

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:17.650
no minor can enter into any legal contract

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.307
without parental consent, including...

00:03:20.307 --> 00:03:21.500
- Prenuptial agreements.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.750
- Prenuptial agreements!

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.450
This contract is void.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:26.750
The fact that my client has been ridden

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.714
more than Seattle Sloo is irrelevant.

00:03:29.714 --> 00:03:33.478
- So this is a very clever legal argument,

00:03:33.478 --> 00:03:36.920
apart from all of the argument

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.870
that Jim Carrey is doing in this scene.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.945
You wouldn't yell it to the courtroom

00:03:41.945 --> 00:03:44.104
during the middle of an examination.

00:03:44.104 --> 00:03:47.222
There are some solid legal
arguments going on here.

00:03:47.222 --> 00:03:50.027
Specifically he's making the argument that

00:03:50.027 --> 00:03:52.956
if a minor enters into a contract,

00:03:52.956 --> 00:03:57.081
the contract is void because
you're not allowed to have

00:03:57.081 --> 00:03:59.339
an adult contract with a minor.

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:02.240
Now that's not exactly
the way the law works.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.671
Generally speaking what happens is,

00:04:04.671 --> 00:04:09.166
a minor can form a contract
without parental consent.

00:04:09.166 --> 00:04:12.201
But what happens is that when the minor

00:04:12.201 --> 00:04:14.239
reaches the age of majority

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:18.910
they have the ability to
either ratify the contract

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:23.140
or to exit the contract
as if it never happened.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:27.104
So, sometimes you really need to be aware

00:04:27.104 --> 00:04:31.470
of a risk when you form
a contract with a minor.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.768
And there's a very famous
case involving Bob Dylan,

00:04:34.768 --> 00:04:38.710
who entered into a studio
contract before he turned 18,

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:42.990
and then I believe he did
not ratify the contract

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:43.823
afterwards.

00:04:43.823 --> 00:04:46.180
But certainly that does not end the case.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.214
And Jim Carrey just can't
simply make an argument

00:04:49.214 --> 00:04:51.630
to the judge and say that therefore

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:54.346
I have won this case because of this

00:04:54.346 --> 00:04:56.395
single piece of evidence.

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:59.420
What would actually happen is that

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:03.170
that would sort of start the
argument in this question.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:06.840
In this case this woman
has been in this marriage

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:08.854
for decades, it seems.

00:05:08.854 --> 00:05:13.496
And of course she enticed her husband

00:05:13.496 --> 00:05:17.830
into entering this prenuptial
agreement through fraud.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:20.845
Now fraud is also often a defense

00:05:20.845 --> 00:05:22.850
to a contract.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:25.238
And there's a doctrine
called Unclean Hands,

00:05:25.238 --> 00:05:28.083
which basically says
that you cannot profit

00:05:28.083 --> 00:05:31.148
from your own deception or deceit

00:05:31.148 --> 00:05:35.400
if you're entering into a contract

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:37.670
or if you have conducted some fraud.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.718
So I don't know where this
particular argument ends up.

00:05:40.718 --> 00:05:43.500
It's a clever argument to
say that she was a minor

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.800
and therefore she has the
ability to disassociate

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.859
the contract itself and rescind it.

00:05:48.859 --> 00:05:52.010
But that is sort of the
beginning of the argument,

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:53.110
not the end.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.650
There are plenty of arguments
the other side can make

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:58.740
to say that, just because she was a minor

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.221
doesn't end the situation.

00:06:00.221 --> 00:06:02.240
And the other person was blameless

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.327
because how could he know that she was

00:06:04.327 --> 00:06:08.130
essentially conducting
a fraud on the marriage?

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:11.660
So there's probably going
to be a lot of analysis

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:15.894
both in marital law and
contract law in California

00:06:15.894 --> 00:06:18.267
to decide which way this one goes.

00:06:18.267 --> 00:06:20.350
It's an interesting argument

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.010
but that's absolutely not the end.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:24.760
It's gonna be litigated
quite a bit from this point.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:27.360
- Standard community property applies,

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.460
and she is entitled to
half of the marital assets

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.493
or $11.395 million.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:34.000
Jordan fades back, swish!

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.314
And that's the game!

00:06:36.314 --> 00:06:38.597
- Yeah, that's not the end of the game.

00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:39.809
That's the beginning.

00:06:39.809 --> 00:06:42.059
(applause)

00:06:45.239 --> 00:06:47.649
(gavel bangs)

00:06:47.649 --> 00:06:49.489
- [Judge] Order.

00:06:49.489 --> 00:06:50.322
Order!

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:54.984
In light of this new evidence,

00:06:54.984 --> 00:06:58.020
the court must rule in favor of Mrs. Cole.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:01.046
She is hereby awarded half
of the marital assets.

00:07:01.046 --> 00:07:05.520
- So, a judge would never
simply issue a ruling

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.160
immediately after one
particular piece of evidence,

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.860
especially when there are
gonna be good legal arguments

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:11.760
going back and forth.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:14.200
In reality the judge would probably ask

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.970
for separate briefing
from both the plaintiff

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:17.864
and the defendant

00:07:17.864 --> 00:07:20.610
on this thorny piece of law,

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:23.490
and to find out what the repercussions are

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:24.930
from this evidence.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.930
That evidence may be dispositive,

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:28.693
but it also may be totally irrelevant.

00:07:28.693 --> 00:07:31.490
So the judge would take
it all into submission

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:34.043
and give it a ruling
within a couple of weeks,

00:07:34.043 --> 00:07:36.567
and not rule from the bench like this.

00:07:36.567 --> 00:07:38.770
The next clip comes from My Cousin Vinny,

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:40.340
one of my favorite legal movies.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:42.760
- Show those eyeglasses
to the jury, please.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:43.593
Thank you.

00:07:46.094 --> 00:07:46.927
Thank you.

00:07:46.927 --> 00:07:50.610
Now, Mr. Tipton, were you
wearing them that day?

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:51.443
- No.

00:07:51.443 --> 00:07:52.519
- Yes, you see...

00:07:52.519 --> 00:07:54.900
You were 50 feet away,

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.015
you made a positive
eyewitness identification,

00:07:57.015 --> 00:08:00.749
and, and, and, and, and yet

00:08:00.749 --> 00:08:03.590
you were not wearing your necessary

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:05.753
prescription eyeglasses.

00:08:06.877 --> 00:08:09.569
- You see this a lot in legal movies

00:08:09.569 --> 00:08:12.579
where the examining attorney

00:08:12.579 --> 00:08:16.103
is literally inches from
the face of the witness.

00:08:16.103 --> 00:08:18.839
In reality you are not
allowed to be anywhere

00:08:18.839 --> 00:08:21.570
near the witness, even a friendly witness.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.280
And the area between the
judge and the witness

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.113
on one hand,

00:08:25.113 --> 00:08:28.850
and the tables for counsel, on the other,

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:31.280
it's called the well, the judicial well.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.872
And you are not allowed to
even set foot in the well

00:08:34.872 --> 00:08:37.678
without the express
permission of the judge.

00:08:37.678 --> 00:08:39.900
So you see that all over the place.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:41.367
You see it in this particular shot.

00:08:41.367 --> 00:08:44.190
He would not be allowed to
be that close to the witness.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:45.853
- They're reading glasses.

00:08:54.176 --> 00:08:55.650
- Uh...

00:08:57.653 --> 00:08:58.960
Well...

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:01.133
Mister...

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:05.900
Could you tell the court

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:07.729
what color eyes

00:09:07.729 --> 00:09:09.903
the defendants have?

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:14.690
- Brown.

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:15.523
Hazel green.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.714
- No more questions.

00:09:22.714 --> 00:09:25.562
- There is a rule amongst trial lawyers

00:09:25.562 --> 00:09:28.430
that you do not ask a
question of the witness

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:29.990
you don't know the answer to,

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:32.080
or that you don't know that the witness

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:34.547
is going to give you a favorable answer.

00:09:34.547 --> 00:09:36.710
That's why in cross examination

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:39.930
you are allowed to
issue leading questions,

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:43.940
which is a question
that implies the answer.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:46.885
And you would almost always
want to use leading questions

00:09:46.885 --> 00:09:49.894
when cross examining a
hostile witness like this one,

00:09:49.894 --> 00:09:54.000
to make sure that you get
the answer that you want.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.490
And often the tone and
substance of the question

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.503
is more important than how
the witness actually answers.

00:10:00.503 --> 00:10:03.230
But he really shot himself in the foot

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:04.754
by asking an open-ended question

00:10:04.754 --> 00:10:07.157
that actually bolstered the credibility

00:10:07.157 --> 00:10:08.819
of the witness on the stand,

00:10:08.819 --> 00:10:11.500
and really hurt his client.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.473
So that's rule number one in
terms of cross examination.

00:10:14.473 --> 00:10:16.273
You don't wanna do that.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:22.080
- [Judge] Mr. Gambini.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:22.913
Your witness.

00:10:24.156 --> 00:10:24.989
- Ha!

00:10:24.989 --> 00:10:25.822
He's a tough one.

00:10:25.822 --> 00:10:26.813
- Yes.

00:10:28.851 --> 00:10:30.605
- Mr. Tipton...

00:10:30.605 --> 00:10:33.507
When you viewed the defendants

00:10:33.507 --> 00:10:36.584
walking from their car
into the Sack of Suds,

00:10:36.584 --> 00:10:39.020
what angle was your point of view?

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:41.190
- They was kinda walkin' toward me

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:42.710
when they entered the store.

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:45.304
- And when they left, what
angle was your point of view?

00:10:45.304 --> 00:10:48.800
- They was kinda walkin' away from me.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:51.480
- So would you say you
got a better shot of them

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.774
goin' in and not so much comin' out?

00:10:53.774 --> 00:10:55.590
- You could say that.

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:56.693
- I did say that.

00:10:56.693 --> 00:10:57.732
Would you say that?

00:10:57.732 --> 00:10:59.000
- Yeah.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:02.276
- That is a good tactic by Joe Pesci here

00:11:02.276 --> 00:11:06.756
to really narrow the witness' testimony,

00:11:06.756 --> 00:11:10.110
and not just generally
agree with the statement

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.845
but to get a positive
statement out of the witness

00:11:11.845 --> 00:11:14.032
along the lines of what you want.

00:11:14.032 --> 00:11:17.423
So he was right to
clarify from the witness.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:23.120
- Is it possible the two "utes"...

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:25.123
- Two what?

00:11:25.123 --> 00:11:28.475
- Also, what Joe Pesci is doing here

00:11:28.475 --> 00:11:31.039
is asking very pointed questions

00:11:31.039 --> 00:11:34.240
that he's not necessarily
going to get an admission

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:35.975
that's going to win his case,

00:11:35.975 --> 00:11:39.191
but he's going to use
that testimony later on

00:11:39.191 --> 00:11:40.596
in the trial.

00:11:40.596 --> 00:11:43.920
And so you have to be very circumspect

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:47.356
with respect to the witnesses
that are on the stand.

00:11:47.356 --> 00:11:50.542
You can't win your entire
case with one witness.

00:11:50.542 --> 00:11:53.570
So all he's doing is
getting the narrow point out

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:57.132
that this witness saw
the two people coming in

00:11:57.132 --> 00:11:59.289
but didn't really see
their faces going out,

00:11:59.289 --> 00:12:01.930
which will be relevant
to what other witnesses

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:04.620
and what other evidence
shows later on in the court.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:06.170
And that's the best that you can do.

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:07.826
And it's your job as the attorney

00:12:07.826 --> 00:12:10.401
to tie that all together
in your closing statements.

00:12:10.401 --> 00:12:12.524
It's not your job to prove your case

00:12:12.524 --> 00:12:14.520
with one particular witness.

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:16.594
So, Joe Pesci is doing
a really good job here.

00:12:16.594 --> 00:12:19.157
- What do you do in your father's garage?

00:12:20.643 --> 00:12:22.520
- Tune ups, oil changes,

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:25.080
brake relining, engine rebuild,

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:26.820
rebuild some trannies, the rear end.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:28.360
- Okay, okay.

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:30.490
But does being an ex-mechanic necessarily

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:33.360
qualify you as being expert on tire marks?

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:34.197
- No.

00:12:34.197 --> 00:12:35.210
Thank you.

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:36.260
Goodbye.

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:39.590
- This is a pretty good back and forth

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:41.041
between the attorney and the witness.

00:12:41.041 --> 00:12:43.740
When you offer a witness as an expert,

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:47.230
the other side is allowed to probe that

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:50.960
and question whether or not
they are sufficient experts

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:53.766
in some area so that they
can offer an opinion.

00:12:53.766 --> 00:12:56.456
The difference between
a person who just tells

00:12:56.456 --> 00:12:58.969
what they know, what they personally saw,

00:12:58.969 --> 00:13:01.450
and an expert, is an expert is allowed

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:04.655
to give an opinion based
on potentially evidence

00:13:04.655 --> 00:13:07.769
but not actually having
personally seen or heard

00:13:07.769 --> 00:13:12.769
or written or whatever the
evidence that's in front of them.

00:13:12.831 --> 00:13:15.490
So that's why it's important to make sure

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:17.337
that they meet the minimum threshold

00:13:17.337 --> 00:13:19.250
for an expert opinion.

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:22.000
And that's what the
attorneys are doing here.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.960
- Your honor, Miss Vito's expertise

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:26.635
is in general automotive knowledge.

00:13:26.635 --> 00:13:29.080
It is in this area that her testimony

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:30.350
will be applicable.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:33.714
Now if Mr. Trotter wishes
to voir dire the witness

00:13:33.714 --> 00:13:36.623
as to the extent of her
expertise in this area,

00:13:36.623 --> 00:13:41.180
I'm sure he's gonna be
more than satisfied.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:43.663
- That's a good retort by Joe Pesci,

00:13:43.663 --> 00:13:48.490
who responds to the
argument that she is not

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:50.200
an expert in tire marks,

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:52.500
but is an expert in a very narrow area.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:54.870
And that is the only area
that he's going to ask

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:56.730
for an expert opinion.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:59.680
Generally this would happen in briefing

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:01.602
and not actually on the witness stand,

00:14:01.602 --> 00:14:04.110
but it could happen on
the witness stand too.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:05.855
And he essentially making a proffer

00:14:05.855 --> 00:14:07.998
that he's only gonna ask certain questions

00:14:07.998 --> 00:14:09.900
of Marisa Tomei.

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:13.080
And the term that he uses, voir dire,

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:15.260
and it's pronounced differently
in different places.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.002
Some places it's called voir "deer".

00:14:17.002 --> 00:14:21.550
That is the correct term
for examining an expert

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:23.730
on their supposed expertise.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:26.692
You would also conduct a
voir "deer", or a voir dire,

00:14:26.692 --> 00:14:30.361
of a jury if you're asking if they are

00:14:30.361 --> 00:14:34.303
sufficient to sit on the jury
in a particular case as well.

00:14:35.752 --> 00:14:37.173
- Okay.

00:14:39.052 --> 00:14:40.690
- All right.

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:41.523
All right.

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:45.550
Now, Ms. Vito,

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:48.703
being an expert on general
automotive knowledge,

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:52.684
can you tell me

00:14:52.684 --> 00:14:56.020
what would the correct ignition timing be

00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:58.870
on a 1955 Bel Air Chevrolet

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:01.444
with a 327 cubic-inch engine

00:15:01.444 --> 00:15:03.621
and a four-barrel carburetor?

00:15:03.621 --> 00:15:05.771
- That's a bullshit question.

00:15:05.771 --> 00:15:08.043
- Does that mean that you can't answer it?

00:15:08.043 --> 00:15:09.530
- It's a bullshit question.

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:10.990
It's impossible to answer.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:13.230
- Impossible because you
don't know the answer!

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:14.810
- Nobody could answer that question.

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.700
- Your honor, I move
to disqualify Ms. Vito

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:19.288
as an expert witness.

00:15:19.288 --> 00:15:21.350
- Can you answer the question?

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:22.183
- No.

00:15:22.183 --> 00:15:23.648
It is a trick question.

00:15:23.648 --> 00:15:25.873
- Why is it a trick question?

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:27.880
- Watch this.

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:30.300
- 'Cause Chevy didn't make a 327 in '55.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:32.380
The 327 didn't come out till '62.

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:33.940
And it wasn't offered in the Bel Air

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:35.971
with a four-barrel carb till '64.

00:15:35.971 --> 00:15:39.695
However, in 1964, the
correct ignition timing

00:15:39.695 --> 00:15:42.143
would be four degrees
before top dead center.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:46.300
- [Trotter] Well...

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:48.396
- Despite the questions
being a little argumentative,

00:15:48.396 --> 00:15:50.890
the big problem there is
that he asked a question

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:52.123
he did not know the answer to,

00:15:52.123 --> 00:15:53.207
and he didn't know how the witness

00:15:53.207 --> 00:15:54.540
was going to answer it,

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:56.380
so he ends up looking like a fool,

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:58.268
and bolstering the
credibility of the witness.

00:15:58.268 --> 00:16:01.222
And while he got a little close

00:16:01.222 --> 00:16:03.817
and was in the well of the court,

00:16:03.817 --> 00:16:07.989
that's, that's an interaction

00:16:07.989 --> 00:16:09.763
that could take place.

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:12.683
- Ah.

00:16:14.632 --> 00:16:16.793
She's acceptable, your honor.

00:16:17.833 --> 00:16:21.150
- And he sees that he's
beaten and he retreats.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:23.720
That's the way a smart lawyer would react.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:27.371
You just move on and you
deal with the testimony

00:16:27.371 --> 00:16:28.860
that comes out later.

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:31.673
This next scene comes from
the movie The Insider.

00:16:32.762 --> 00:16:33.850
- That is correct.

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:35.043
- In other words it acts as a drug.

00:16:35.043 --> 00:16:36.700
- I object to the form of the question.

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:37.709
- It acts as a drug on the...

00:16:37.709 --> 00:16:38.741
- Object to the form...

00:16:38.741 --> 00:16:39.776
- It acts as a drug...

00:16:39.776 --> 00:16:40.609
- Object!

00:16:40.609 --> 00:16:42.100
- Is there an echo in here?

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:43.645
Your objection's been recorded.

00:16:43.645 --> 00:16:46.020
She typed it into her
little machine over there.

00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:46.870
It's on the record.

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:49.760
- This is a really interesting scene

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:52.123
that you really don't
see much of in the media.

00:16:52.123 --> 00:16:53.700
This is a deposition,

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:57.130
so you are allowed to ask questions

00:16:57.130 --> 00:16:58.278
of a particular witness.

00:16:58.278 --> 00:17:00.960
The witness has to give
their testimony under oath,

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:02.811
and it's all recorded by a stenographer

00:17:02.811 --> 00:17:05.320
as if that witness was on the stand.

00:17:05.320 --> 00:17:07.292
But this takes place before trial.

00:17:07.292 --> 00:17:10.680
This is not a part of
the trial proceedings,

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:12.474
it's part of the discovery proceedings,

00:17:12.474 --> 00:17:14.418
before trail ever happens.

00:17:14.418 --> 00:17:16.790
So what generally happens
in a deposition is

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:20.131
you have the witness, the
attorney for the witness,

00:17:20.131 --> 00:17:22.100
the attorney for the other side,

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:24.383
and the stenographer to
write down every word

00:17:24.383 --> 00:17:25.720
that the witness says.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:29.103
And that becomes part of
the deposition record.

00:17:29.103 --> 00:17:31.647
And the opposing attorney is allowed

00:17:31.647 --> 00:17:35.644
to make objections to the questions

00:17:35.644 --> 00:17:36.817
that you're asking.

00:17:36.817 --> 00:17:39.270
But nothing really happens.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:41.406
You're just objecting
so that you can preserve

00:17:41.406 --> 00:17:43.014
that objection for a later time

00:17:43.014 --> 00:17:46.024
in case the question
is ever asked in court.

00:17:46.024 --> 00:17:48.251
If you fail to make your objection

00:17:48.251 --> 00:17:49.900
during a disposition,

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:51.660
and the same question
is asked on the stand,

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:53.550
you have waived your objection

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:55.670
and you can't object at a later time.

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:57.306
So you're preserving the record.

00:17:57.306 --> 00:18:00.350
What's going on here is
something that happens rarely

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:01.340
but it does happen,

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:04.029
which is where you have this giant room

00:18:04.029 --> 00:18:06.620
because it's a huge class action,

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:08.190
and you have an attorney for one side

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:10.804
and then hundreds or dozens of attorneys

00:18:10.804 --> 00:18:12.600
for the other side.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:15.337
In this case The Insider
is the real life story

00:18:15.337 --> 00:18:20.075
of the tobacco litigation that
happened in the early '90s.

00:18:20.075 --> 00:18:23.021
So the tobacco industry
obviously has an army

00:18:23.021 --> 00:18:26.740
of lawyers, and they're
trying to stop this witness

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:27.600
from testifying.

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:29.360
So he's making his objection,

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.457
and he specifically says,
I object to the form

00:18:31.457 --> 00:18:34.320
of the question, which in the south

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.856
is generally the only
thing you're allowed to do

00:18:36.856 --> 00:18:38.460
in a deposition.

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:39.820
You can't object to hearsay.

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:41.235
You can't object to speculation.

00:18:41.235 --> 00:18:44.410
You can only object to
the form of the question.

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:45.610
So that's what he's doing,

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:47.710
but he's doing it over
and over and over again

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:50.000
to try and prevent the
witness from testifying.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:51.844
So it's a common tactic.

00:18:51.844 --> 00:18:54.149
It's not exactly what you should be doing,

00:18:54.149 --> 00:18:56.750
but it's true to life.

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:58.860
So this could be the actual dialogue

00:18:58.860 --> 00:19:00.350
from a real life deposition.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:01.335
I'm not sure.

00:19:01.335 --> 00:19:04.400
But stuff like that definitely happens.

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:08.205
- So now I'll proceed with
my deposition of my witness.

00:19:08.205 --> 00:19:09.381
- That's right.

00:19:09.381 --> 00:19:12.420
The opposing side has
made their objections.

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:15.000
They've objected to the form
of the question many times.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:17.870
And then the other
attorney just moves forward

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:19.100
and says, you made your objection,

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:20.460
you preserved for the record.

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:22.903
Now I'm moving on and
I'm asking my question.

00:19:24.792 --> 00:19:25.625
- Does it...

00:19:25.625 --> 00:19:26.875
- Dr. Wigand...

00:19:28.315 --> 00:19:30.657
I am instructing you

00:19:30.657 --> 00:19:33.570
not to answer that question.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.390
In accordance to the
terms of the contractual

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:39.230
obligations undertaken by you

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:42.250
not to disclose any information

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:45.520
about your work at the
Brown and Williamson

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:46.610
tobacco company.

00:19:46.610 --> 00:19:49.810
- So he has instructed
the witness not to answer.

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:52.250
And that usually comes up
only when you're dealing

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:53.890
with a privilege issue.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:57.160
So, for example, if you ask a question

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:59.858
that infringes on the
attorney/client privilege,

00:19:59.858 --> 00:20:02.530
than you would instruct
the witness not to answer.

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:05.135
So an opposing attorney
can't ask, for example,

00:20:05.135 --> 00:20:07.330
what you discussed with your attorney.

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:10.380
In that situation you
would instruct your client

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.450
not to answer on the
basis of that privilege.

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:14.450
This is a different situation, though,

00:20:14.450 --> 00:20:17.370
because the tobacco company is effectively

00:20:17.370 --> 00:20:18.470
a third party here.

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:22.914
So this doctor no longer works
for the tobacco companies.

00:20:22.914 --> 00:20:27.470
So they probably can't instruct someone

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:29.780
who is a third party
to them not to answer.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:31.795
Usually it's up to the witness

00:20:31.795 --> 00:20:33.740
whether they're going to follow

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:35.410
the attorney's instructions or not.

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:38.813
And so it's a little
tenuous whether you can

00:20:38.813 --> 00:20:41.510
instruct a witness not
to answer on the basis

00:20:41.510 --> 00:20:43.250
of a contractual obligation.

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:46.223
And by the way, usually
nondisclosure agreements

00:20:46.223 --> 00:20:49.330
don't hold up in the face
of a court proceeding,

00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:52.931
because then that would be
contrary to public policy.

00:20:52.931 --> 00:20:56.611
So using a nondisclosure agreement

00:20:56.611 --> 00:20:59.379
to try and prevent a
witness from testifying

00:20:59.379 --> 00:21:01.990
is an interesting
tactic, but I don't think

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:02.823
it would hold up.

00:21:02.823 --> 00:21:06.093
- And in accordance with
the force and effect

00:21:06.093 --> 00:21:08.650
of the temporary restraining order

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:10.409
that has been entered against you

00:21:10.409 --> 00:21:13.283
by the court in the state of Kentucky.

00:21:14.374 --> 00:21:16.780
That means you don't talk.

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:19.982
- So that's another
situation that's interesting

00:21:19.982 --> 00:21:23.130
in that there's supposedly
a restraining order

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:24.575
from another state,

00:21:24.575 --> 00:21:28.149
ordering him not to disclose.

00:21:28.149 --> 00:21:30.330
It's an interesting situation.

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:31.908
And I know that in real life this was

00:21:31.908 --> 00:21:33.655
a big part of this litigation

00:21:33.655 --> 00:21:35.752
whether this former doctor,

00:21:35.752 --> 00:21:38.650
or this doctor who was a former employee

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:40.908
of the tobacco company, was bound by this

00:21:40.908 --> 00:21:42.527
nondisclosure agreement.

00:21:42.527 --> 00:21:45.613
But this interchange
between the two attorneys

00:21:45.613 --> 00:21:48.127
and the objections and the instructions

00:21:48.127 --> 00:21:52.860
not to answer, that dialogue
is pretty realistic.

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:55.450
And sometimes you get these big rooms.

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:57.760
This is a little bit more dramatic

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:01.010
than you would have in a real deposition.

00:22:01.010 --> 00:22:03.532
Even in one that has dozens of attorneys,

00:22:03.532 --> 00:22:05.864
you're really just in
a big conference room

00:22:05.864 --> 00:22:07.730
sitting around at a table.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:11.208
No one is standing up walking
around asking questions,

00:22:11.208 --> 00:22:14.342
you know, pretending like you're in court.

00:22:14.342 --> 00:22:17.768
It's all just everybody sitting down,

00:22:17.768 --> 00:22:20.439
and you're not gonna get
these sort of histrionics.

00:22:20.439 --> 00:22:23.000
But the dialogue itself is true to life,

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:24.620
and in these big class actions

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:26.540
you can get situations where you have

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:28.600
lots of parties in a deposition.

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:30.750
And sometimes it can
get pretty heated too.

00:22:31.805 --> 00:22:34.030
- Mr. Motley, we have rights here.

00:22:34.030 --> 00:22:35.303
- Oh, you got rights.

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:38.160
And lefts.

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:40.050
Ups and downs and middles.

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:41.115
So what?

00:22:41.115 --> 00:22:44.245
You don't get to instruct
anything around here.

00:22:44.245 --> 00:22:46.480
This is not North Carolina,

00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:49.224
not South Carolina, nor Kentucky.

00:22:49.224 --> 00:22:52.800
This is the sovereign state
of Mississippi's proceeding.

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:55.107
Wipe that smirk off your face!

00:22:55.107 --> 00:22:58.943
Dr. Wigand's deposition
will be part of this record!

00:22:58.943 --> 00:23:01.385
And I'm gonna take my witness' testimony

00:23:01.385 --> 00:23:04.128
whether the hell you like it or not!

00:23:04.128 --> 00:23:07.071
- Yeah, and so there you have it.

00:23:07.071 --> 00:23:10.008
He's saying that the
other side can't instruct

00:23:10.008 --> 00:23:11.520
the witness not to answer.

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:13.128
And in the absence of a court order,

00:23:13.128 --> 00:23:14.950
in this particular court,

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:17.040
that attorney is doing the right thing

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:18.210
and he's just moving forward.

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:20.670
And the attorneys can argue on whether

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:23.373
that testimony is
admissible at a later date,

00:23:23.373 --> 00:23:26.628
but the important thing is
to get that testimony down

00:23:26.628 --> 00:23:29.400
on paper so that you at
least have the option

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:32.906
to use it later regardless
of whether it was proper

00:23:32.906 --> 00:23:34.040
in the first place.

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:37.435
And when you get these
disputes in a deposition,

00:23:37.435 --> 00:23:40.242
you have to, it's a
game of chicken usually.

00:23:40.242 --> 00:23:42.560
The other side will say
that they're gonna shut

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:43.780
the deposition down,

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:46.331
and potentially face the ire of the court,

00:23:46.331 --> 00:23:48.340
unless they can convince the court

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:50.900
that what the other side
was doing was so crazy

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:53.380
that the deposition
shouldn't move forward.

00:23:53.380 --> 00:23:56.025
So you gotta be very
careful on both sides,

00:23:56.025 --> 00:23:58.723
and when in doubt just move forward

00:23:58.723 --> 00:24:01.760
and ask the questions so
you can get some testimony

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:03.097
on the record.

00:24:03.097 --> 00:24:06.103
All right, the next clip is
from The Devil's Advocate.

00:24:06.103 --> 00:24:08.642
- [Man] All right, Mr.
Clinteen, let me ask you this.

00:24:08.642 --> 00:24:10.770
Do you think as a juror

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:13.270
you would be able to set
aside any prior opinion

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:15.820
you might hold about the
savings and loan industry?

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:19.160
That was a question, sir.

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:20.070
- What?

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:20.970
Do I like bankers?

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:27.650
- This is a voir dire
of a panel of jurors.

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:30.542
A lot of people will
experience this very thing

00:24:30.542 --> 00:24:33.105
when they get called for jury duty.

00:24:33.105 --> 00:24:36.660
One side is asking
questions of the jurors.

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:38.810
They're trying to figure out, number one,

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:40.440
can they be impartial, but number two,

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.303
more importantly, are they more likely

00:24:42.303 --> 00:24:44.705
to vote for your side over the other's?

00:24:44.705 --> 00:24:47.716
And the attorney has
asked a classic question

00:24:47.716 --> 00:24:51.456
about can you be impartial
for a particular defendant

00:24:51.456 --> 00:24:52.599
or plaintiff?

00:24:52.599 --> 00:24:55.615
And the next best thing to getting someone

00:24:55.615 --> 00:24:59.110
who you think is going to
vote for you in the trial

00:24:59.110 --> 00:25:02.390
is to show that they can't be impartial

00:25:02.390 --> 00:25:04.073
and have the judge dismiss them.

00:25:04.073 --> 00:25:08.240
So you get a certain number
of peremptory challenges,

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:11.820
which means that you can
get rid of potential jurors

00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:13.000
for no reason at all,

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:15.230
but you only get a
certain amount of those.

00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:17.670
But if you can convince the judge

00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:20.020
to get rid of the jurors for you,

00:25:20.020 --> 00:25:22.135
then you get an unlimited number of those.

00:25:22.135 --> 00:25:24.290
So it's a game of cat and mouse

00:25:24.290 --> 00:25:28.132
between the attorneys, the
jurors and the other side.

00:25:28.132 --> 00:25:31.132
(man clears throat)

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:34.700
- Your honor, may I have a minute, please,

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:35.820
to confer with my colleagues?

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:36.653
- [Judge] You may.

00:25:40.525 --> 00:25:41.358
- Neville.

00:25:41.358 --> 00:25:42.870
While you're at it, let's
get rid of number four,

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:44.950
six, and I'd say lose number 12,

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:47.840
except the prosecutor's gonna
fuck up and do it for us.

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:48.673
- Number six?

00:25:50.648 --> 00:25:53.519
- In a major case, you actually might have

00:25:53.519 --> 00:25:56.111
jury consultants behind you.

00:25:56.111 --> 00:25:58.426
It's very common to consult with not only

00:25:58.426 --> 00:26:00.902
your co-counsel, who
are watching the jurors

00:26:00.902 --> 00:26:02.403
while you're asking questions,

00:26:02.403 --> 00:26:05.320
and they might have some intuition

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:07.860
about who they should keep and
who they should get rid of.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.612
But you also might have consultants

00:26:10.612 --> 00:26:13.907
who all they do all day long

00:26:13.907 --> 00:26:18.320
is try to pick favorable juries for you.

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:21.195
So there aren't any consultants here

00:26:21.195 --> 00:26:24.860
because they're relying on
the acumen of Keanu Reeves.

00:26:24.860 --> 00:26:27.470
But you might see one in real life

00:26:27.470 --> 00:26:28.470
if the case is big enough.

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:30.703
If the dollar amount is worth it.

00:26:32.019 --> 00:26:33.820
- You're kiddin', right?

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:34.980
She's my first choice.

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:36.345
- She's my first pass.

00:26:36.345 --> 00:26:37.433
- And four?

00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:42.915
With the dreadlocks.

00:26:42.915 --> 00:26:44.126
That's crazy.

00:26:44.126 --> 00:26:46.423
That's a defendant's
juror if I ever saw one.

00:26:46.423 --> 00:26:48.858
- Did you see his shoes?

00:26:48.858 --> 00:26:51.547
- Yeah, it's unfortunate
that there is a lot

00:26:51.547 --> 00:26:54.490
of sort of stereotyping that goes on

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:56.235
when you're selecting a jury.

00:26:56.235 --> 00:26:58.850
I think that's sort of what
they're alluding to here

00:26:58.850 --> 00:27:02.962
with that particular pick, or dismissal.

00:27:02.962 --> 00:27:05.863
There are laws that say
you cannot get rid of,

00:27:05.863 --> 00:27:10.140
you know, every minority
that's on a jury, for example.

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:14.670
You've gotta thread a very fine line

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:15.860
in some of these cases.

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:18.120
So it's unfortunate that
they're sort of profiling

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:19.430
people based on that.

00:27:19.430 --> 00:27:22.380
But it happens in real life unfortunately.

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:25.253
- Look, kid, maybe down in Florida

00:27:25.253 --> 00:27:27.130
you are the next big thing.

00:27:27.130 --> 00:27:28.890
This is New York, Manhattan.

00:27:28.890 --> 00:27:30.720
We're not squeezing oranges here.

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:32.722
- He polishes those shoes every night.

00:27:32.722 --> 00:27:34.648
He makes his own clothes.

00:27:34.648 --> 00:27:36.921
He may look like a brother
with an attitude to you,

00:27:36.921 --> 00:27:39.655
but I see a man with a
shotgun under his bed.

00:27:39.655 --> 00:27:43.423
And woe betide the creature
who steps into his garden.

00:27:43.423 --> 00:27:47.120
And number six, your favorite?

00:27:47.120 --> 00:27:48.594
She's damaged goods.

00:27:48.594 --> 00:27:51.340
- There's a science to
picking a good jury,

00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:53.779
but there's also a lot of
intuition that goes on too.

00:27:53.779 --> 00:27:57.202
I don't know that it would be so crazy

00:27:57.202 --> 00:27:59.560
as what Keanu Reeves
is talking about here,

00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:01.591
but there's an art and a science to it.

00:28:01.591 --> 00:28:03.793
It can really make a
difference in your case.

00:28:03.793 --> 00:28:06.440
So those are some of my
favorite legal movies.

